Digitally reconfigured cultural dependencies
1. Shared verification culture for AI-derived construction knowledge (data and software count 32).
   NLP accident classification and automated bid preparation made safety reports and prior proposals reusable, but ambiguous terminology, incomplete data and fragmented documentation limited output credibility (c008;c009). The cultural dependency is not data quality itself; it is the shared expectation that AI outputs must be questioned, contextually interpreted and reviewed by experienced personnel. Human–machine trust becomes unsafe where algorithmic output is treated as authoritative rather than contestable.
2. Practice-oriented safety-learning culture around XR (instructional count 25).
   AR and VR replaced passive construction briefings with hazard rehearsal and consequence feedback (c002;c005). Yet some systems transmitted only explicit knowledge, and improved test scores did not automatically establish workplace application or long-term retention (c004;c011). XR therefore works where the safety culture values rehearsal, reflection and discussion of mistakes—not where immersive training is treated as another compliance exercise.
3. Digital disclosure trust in narrative, bid and project repositories (governance-constraint count 16).
   GenAI bid systems introduced confidentiality concerns when documents passed through external APIs, while narrative repositories contained sensitive accounts of project errors and interpersonal events (c009;c012). Digital codification was conditional on culturally credible assurances about consent, anonymization, access and withdrawal. Employees will not externalize tacit knowledge when repository participation feels exposing or punitive.
Persistently established cultural dependencies
1. Participative construction leadership versus authoritarian adoption (culture and adoption-barrier count 23; leadership count 10).
   Authoritarian and abusive leadership reduced knowledge sharing, delayed AI adoption and left predictive tools underused (c014;c014). Participative leadership instead supported stakeholder involvement, training and proactive intervention (c008;c014). Leadership style therefore determined whether technology served collective learning or managerial surveillance.
2. Collective stewardship across project turnover and retirement (knowledge-governance count 16).
   Small contractors lost project expertise when employees departed, while temporary consultancy teams dispersed after assignments (c007;c012). Heritage craft and OHS cases showed similar risks when practitioners or project staff changed (c001;c016). Repositories retained knowledge only where stewardship—capturing, updating and reusing lessons—was understood as a continuing collective responsibility.
3. Psychological safety, openness and contribution motivation (culture and relational-climate count 10; incentive count 1).
Storytelling required trust because practitioners disclosed mistakes, judgment calls and sensitive experiences (c012;c012). Knowledge hoarding, job-security fears and competitive attitudes discouraged contribution even when a platform existed (c007;c012). Incentives could support participation, but they could not replace psychological safety or confidence that sharing would not damage employment or status.
All retained insights now identify cultural mechanisms. Costs, infrastructure, workload and interface limitations remain relevant boundary conditions, but they are not labelled as organizational culture.